{
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Lab Exercise: Feature Scaling and Transformation\n",
        "This notebook explores who needs feature scaling, how different scalers handle extreme outliers, how log/power transformations resolve skewness, and the true impact of preprocessing leakage.\n",
        "\n",
        "### Learning Objectives\n",
        "1. **Model Sensitivity**: Quantify the impact of feature scaling on Distance-based (kNN/SVM), Linear (Logistic Regression), and Tree-based (Random Forest) models.\n",
        "2. **Outlier Poisoning**: Measure how a single extreme outlier affects standard, min-max, and robust scalers.\n",
        "3. **Sparsity Preservation**: Learn how to safely scale sparse matrices without exhausting memory.\n",
        "4. **Distribution Skew**: Correct skewed distributions using fixed log/sqrt functions and fitted PowerTransformers.\n",
        "5. **Data Isolation**: Measure the exact impact of leakage when scaling before a train-test split."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Setup & Environment Initialization"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "import warnings\n",
        "import numpy as np\n",
        "import pandas as pd\n",
        "from scipy import stats\n",
        "from sklearn.datasets import fetch_california_housing, load_breast_cancer\n",
        "from sklearn.ensemble import RandomForestClassifier\n",
        "from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n",
        "from sklearn.model_selection import cross_val_score, train_test_split\n",
        "from sklearn.neighbors import KNeighborsClassifier, KNeighborsRegressor\n",
        "from sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\n",
        "from sklearn.preprocessing import (\n",
        "    MaxAbsScaler,\n",
        "    MinMaxScaler,\n",
        "    Normalizer,\n",
        "    PowerTransformer,\n",
        "    QuantileTransformer,\n",
        "    RobustScaler,\n",
        "    StandardScaler\n",
        ")\n",
        "from sklearn.svm import SVC\n",
        "from sklearn.compose import ColumnTransformer\n",
        "\n",
        "warnings.filterwarnings(\"ignore\")\n",
        "print(\"✓ Environment initialized successfully.\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 1. The Scale Problem & Model Sensitivity\n",
        "Let's see how much different models care about features being on wildly different scales. We'll use the Breast Cancer dataset where features have std deviations ranging from 0.002 to 569.36 (a 215,171x spread ratio)."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "d = load_breast_cancer(as_frame=True)\n",
        "X, y = d.data, d.target\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Breast Cancer Dataset - Sample Feature Spreads:\")\n",
        "print(pd.DataFrame({\"min\": X.min(), \"max\": X.max(), \"std\": X.std()}).loc[\n",
        "    [\"mean area\", \"worst area\", \"mean radius\", \"mean smoothness\"]\n",
        "].round(4).to_string())\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"\\nlargest std {X.std().max():.2f} ({X.std().idxmax()}), \"\n",
        "      f\"smallest {X.std().min():.6f} ({X.std().idxmin()})\")\n",
        "print(f\"ratio: {X.std().max() / X.std().min():,.0f}x\\n\")\n",
        "\n",
        "print(f\"   {'model':<24}{'unscaled':>10}{'scaled':>9}{'gain':>9}\")\n",
        "for name, mk in [\n",
        "        (\"KNeighbors(5)\", lambda: KNeighborsClassifier()),\n",
        "        (\"SVC(rbf)\", lambda: SVC()),\n",
        "        (\"LogisticRegression\", lambda: LogisticRegression(max_iter=500)),\n",
        "        (\"RandomForest\", lambda: RandomForestClassifier(random_state=0))]:\n",
        "    raw = cross_val_score(mk(), X, y, cv=5).mean()\n",
        "    sc = cross_val_score(make_pipeline(StandardScaler(), mk()), X, y, cv=5).mean()\n",
        "    print(f\"   {name:<24}{raw:>10.4f}{sc:>9.4f}{sc - raw:>+9.4f}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 2. One Outlier, Four Scalers\n",
        "Let's see how a single outlier (`5000.0` inside a group of 200 values around `50`) affects StandardScaler, MinMaxScaler, RobustScaler, and MaxAbsScaler. Read the `span` of the 200 real values - that is, how much of the range they got to keep."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "rng = np.random.RandomState(0)\n",
        "data = np.append(rng.normal(50, 10, 200), 5000.0).reshape(-1, 1)\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"ONE OUTLIER - 200 values around 50, plus a single 5000\")\n",
        "print(f\"   {'scaler':<18}{'uses':<16}{'range of the 200':>20}{'span':>9}\")\n",
        "for name, sc, uses in [\n",
        "        (\"StandardScaler\", StandardScaler(), \"mean / std\"),\n",
        "        (\"MinMaxScaler\", MinMaxScaler(), \"min / max\"),\n",
        "        (\"RobustScaler\", RobustScaler(), \"median / IQR\"),\n",
        "        (\"MaxAbsScaler\", MaxAbsScaler(), \"max absolute\")]:\n",
        "    body = sc.fit_transform(data).ravel()[:200]\n",
        "    print(f\"   {name:<18}{uses:<16}\"\n",
        "          f\"{f'{body.min():.4f} .. {body.max():.4f}':>20}{body.max() - body.min():>9.4f}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 3. Normalizer Scales Rows, Not Columns\n",
        "Let's visually prove that `Normalizer` is NOT a feature scaler - it rescales rows independently so that each sample vector has unit length."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "m = np.array([[1.0, 2.0, 3.0], [100.0, 200.0, 300.0]])\n",
        "print(f\"   input rows      {m[0].tolist()} and {m[1].tolist()}\")\n",
        "print(f\"   StandardScaler  {StandardScaler().fit_transform(m).round(3).tolist()}\")\n",
        "print(f"   Normalizer      {Normalizer().fit_transform(m).round(3).tolist()}\")\n",
        "print(\"\\n-> Notice that Normalizer discarded magnitude entirely and made the two rows IDENTICAL.\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 4. Transformations Against Skewness\n",
        "When data is heavily right-skewed, standard scaling doesn't resolve the asymmetry. Let's compare log transforms, square roots, PowerTransformer (Yeo-Johnson), and QuantileTransformer on some housing features."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "ca = fetch_california_housing(as_frame=True).frame\n",
        "print(\"SKEWNESS COMPARISON (0 = symmetric):\")\n",
        "print(f\"   {'column':<12}{'raw':>9}{'log1p':>9}{'sqrt':>9}{'Yeo-Johnson':>13}{'Quantile':>10}\")\n",
        "for c in [\"MedInc\", \"AveRooms\", \"AveOccup\", \"Population\"]:\n",
        "    v = ca[c].values.reshape(-1, 1)\n",
        "    yj = PowerTransformer().fit_transform(v).ravel()\n",
        "    qt = QuantileTransformer(output_distribution=\"normal\", n_quantiles=1000,\n",
        "                             random_state=0).fit_transform(v).ravel()\n",
        "    print(f\"   {c:<12}{stats.skew(v.ravel()):>9.2f}{stats.skew(np.log1p(v.ravel())):>9.2f}\"\n",
        "          f\"{stats.skew(np.sqrt(v.ravel())):>9.2f}{stats.skew(yj):>13.2f}{stats.skew(qt):>10.2f}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## 5. Scaling Before the Split Leakage\n",
        "Let's measure the exact magnitude of information leakage when scaling parameters are computed over the full dataset before splitting."
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": null,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [],
      "source": [
        "Xb, yb = load_breast_cancer(return_X_y=True)\n",
        "for label, Scaler in [(\"StandardScaler\", StandardScaler), (\"MinMaxScaler\", MinMaxScaler)]:\n",
        "    honest, leaked = [], []\n",
        "    for seed in range(30):\n",
        "        Xtr, Xte, ytr, yte = train_test_split(Xb, yb, test_size=0.3,\n",
        "                                              random_state=seed, stratify=yb)\n",
        "        sc = Scaler().fit(Xtr)\n",
        "        honest.append(KNeighborsClassifier().fit(sc.transform(Xtr), ytr)\n",
        "                      .score(sc.transform(Xte), yte))\n",
        "        \n",
        "        Xall = Scaler().fit_transform(Xb)\n",
        "        Atr, Ate, btr, bte = train_test_split(Xall, yb, test_size=0.3,\n",
        "                                              random_state=seed, stratify=yb)\n",
        "        leaked.append(KNeighborsClassifier().fit(Atr, btr).score(Ate, bte))\n",
        "    print(f\"   {label:<16}honest {np.mean(honest):.4f}   leaked {np.mean(leaked):.4f}\"\n",
        "          f\"   inflation {np.mean(leaked) - np.mean(honest):+.4f}\")"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Hands-On Programming Exercises"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
